---
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
import home from '../../assets/projects/union-website/home.png';
import membersApp from '../../assets/projects/union-website/members-app.png';
import apprenticeship from '../../assets/projects/union-website/apprenticeship.png';
import contractors from '../../assets/projects/union-website/contractors.png';
import calendar from '../../assets/projects/union-website/calendar.png';
import locals from '../../assets/projects/union-website/locals.png';
import local from '../../assets/projects/union-website/local.png';
import signin from '../../assets/projects/union-website/signin.png';
import healthWelfare from '../../assets/projects/union-website/health-welfare.png';
import phoneHome from '../../assets/projects/union-website/phone-home.png';
import phoneMenu from '../../assets/projects/union-website/phone-menu.png';
import phoneCalendar from '../../assets/projects/union-website/phone-calendar.png';

const desktopSizes = '(min-width: 1000px) 960px, calc(100vw - 32px)';
const pairSizes = '(min-width: 1000px) 470px, (min-width: 900px) 45vw, calc(100vw - 32px)';
const phoneSizes = '(min-width: 760px) 230px, (min-width: 600px) 30vw, 64vw';

const pages = [
  { name: 'Home', text: 'Who the council is, and a slide for each part of the platform behind the site.' },
  { name: 'Members', text: 'The member app, how members organize together, and the path from apprentice to journeyman.' },
  { name: 'Contractors', text: 'Why a contractor partners with the union, what the contractor portal does, and where its view of the shared record stops.' },
  { name: 'Health & Welfare', text: 'How hours worked become health coverage, and what to do after an injury on the job.' },
  { name: 'Calendar', text: 'Holidays, scholarship deadlines, and fundraisers, month by month.' },
  { name: 'Contact and the nine locals', text: 'The council office, a short written-enquiry form, and a card for every affiliated local, each with its own page.' },
  { name: 'About and Resources', text: 'The council’s affiliations and the international union, plus a directory of training funds, safety agencies, and voter registration in English and Spanish.' },
  { name: 'Sign in', text: 'Separate sign-ins for members, union staff, and contractors, with password reset for members.' },
];

const phoneScreens = [
  { src: phoneHome, alt: 'The home page on a phone, with the welcome headline and the button to download the members app' },
  { src: phoneMenu, alt: 'The mobile navigation menu open, with five pages and the sign-in and contact buttons' },
  { src: phoneCalendar, alt: 'The calendar on a phone, showing November with two holidays marked and listed below the month' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  name="Labor Union Website"
  description="Case study: the public website Sām Kazemi designed and built in Next.js for a district council of labor unions and its nine affiliated locals, at Workforce Systems."
  summary="The public website for a district council of labor unions and its nine affiliated locals: one front door for members, contractors, and the community, built in Next.js alongside the workforce platform it signs people into."
  role="Lead Developer: design and front end"
  org="Workforce Systems (WFS)"
  period="Aug 2026 – Present"
  status="Built; launch planned"
  accent="lapis"
  stack={[
    'Next.js (App Router)',
    'React',
    'TypeScript',
    'Tailwind CSS',
    'Responsive design',
    'Accessible UI',
    'Playwright',
    'Design systems',
    'Information architecture',
  ]}
>
  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p>
    <h2>One front door for a council of nine locals</h2>
    <p>
      A district council speaks for several local unions at once, and its website is read by very
      different people. Members want to know where they stand and which hall is theirs. Contractors
      want to know why to hire union labor and how to ask for a crew. Neighbors and future members
      want to know who the council is and how to reach it. Each of them needs an answer in a page or
      two, and many will be reading on a phone.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is also the public face of the{' '}
      <a href={url('/projects/workforce-systems')}>Union Workforce Ecosystem</a>, the platform
      Workforce Systems is building for the same union. Sām leads both, and the website lives in
      the same Next.js app, so signing in moves a member, a staff member, or a contractor
      straight into their portal.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={home}
        alt="The website home page: a welcome headline over a photo of a construction crew, with buttons to download the members app or learn more"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The home page. The council’s name, logo, locals, addresses, phone numbers, and officers are
        replaced with placeholders in every screen on this page.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The site</p>
    <h2>Every page answers one question</h2>
    <p>
      Every page answers one reader’s question, and no answer is more than a click from the top bar
      or the footer.
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-flow">
      {
        pages.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <div>
              <strong>{p.name}</strong>
              <span>{p.text}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">One council, nine locals</p>
    <h2>Find your hall</h2>
    <p>
      Dispatch happens at each local’s own hall, so the contact page lists all nine with their
      address and phone, and each card opens a page for that local: its office, branch offices,
      hours, dispatch window, meetings, and officers. All nine pages come from one table and are
      generated at build time, so adding a local is one entry, and a local that isn’t in the table
      simply has no page.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot cs-shot--wide">
      <div class="cs-pair">
        <Screenshot
          src={locals}
          alt="The contact page’s grid of affiliated local unions, each card with an address, phone number, and a More information link"
          widths={[600, 900, 1400]}
          sizes={pairSizes}
          class="cs-screen"
        />
        <Screenshot
          src={local}
          alt="One local’s own page: the hall’s office, phone, website, office hours, dispatch window, and meetings"
          widths={[600, 900, 1400]}
          sizes={pairSizes}
          class="cs-screen"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption>The nine affiliated locals on the contact page, and one local’s own page.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Members</p>
    <h2>The app, and the path into the trade</h2>
    <p>
      The members page explains the app in terms of what a member looks up: book position, dispatch
      ticket, card wallet, hours, dues, and coverage. Beside the copy is the member app’s actual
      home screen running inside a phone frame rather than a picture of one, so the site and the app
      can’t drift apart.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={membersApp}
        alt="The members page: six things the member app shows, the app store buttons, and the member app’s home screen in a phone frame"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>The members page, with the member app’s live home screen beside the copy.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Further down, the apprenticeship section lays out the five steps from the entry skill
      assessment to journeyman certification, next to the training center that houses and feeds
      trainees at no cost to them or their employer.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={apprenticeship}
        alt="The apprenticeship section: five numbered steps from passing the skill assessment to certifying as a journeyman, beside a training center card"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>Five steps from apprentice to journeyman, and the training center behind them.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Contractors</p>
    <h2>The case for hiring union</h2>
    <p>
      The contractors page opens with why a contractor should partner with the union at all: a
      trained workforce, apprenticeship, competitive wage rates, prevailing-wage savings, and six
      more services. They sit in a carousel that uses the browser’s own scroll snapping, so swipes,
      trackpads, and arrow keys all work, with a custom scrollbar drawn underneath that can be
      dragged. The page then explains what the contractor portal does and where its view of the
      shared record stops.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={contractors}
        alt="The contractors page: three service cards in a carousel, covering the workforce, training and apprenticeship, and wage rates, above a drawn scrollbar"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>Ten services in a carousel, three at a time on a desktop.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Calendar</p>
    <h2>A calendar that keeps California time</h2>
    <p>
      The calendar holds council holidays, scholarship deadlines, and fundraisers. It runs on
      Pacific Time whatever the reader’s own clock says, so the month and the upcoming list both
      turn over at midnight in California. Clicking a day narrows the list to that day’s events,
      and an upcoming-events list below always shows what’s next.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={calendar}
        alt="The calendar on November 2026, with two holiday events marked on the 26th and 27th and listed under This month"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>The calendar on November, with its holidays marked and listed below the month.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Health and welfare</p>
    <h2>Benefits explained in plain language</h2>
    <p>
      Union health coverage is earned through hours worked, and that is rarely explained well. The
      Health &amp; Welfare page walks through it in four steps: you work the hours, the contractor
      pays the trust, your hours build an eligibility bank, and a monthly run checks the bank
      against the plan minimum. It then covers other plans a member can connect, and what to do
      after an injury on the job.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={healthWelfare}
        alt="The Health and Welfare page: Hours in, coverage out, with four numbered steps from working the hours to the monthly eligibility run"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>How coverage is earned, in four steps.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Signing in</p>
    <h2>Three kinds of account, one front door</h2>
    <p>
      Members, union staff, and contractors each have their own sign-in on the website, and each
      one links to the other two. Sign-in runs against the platform’s Laravel API. The token is kept
      in an httpOnly cookie the browser never reads, “remember this device” decides whether it
      outlives the session, and a member who forgets a password gets an emailed link back to the
      site’s own reset page.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={signin}
        alt="The website sign-in page: email and password fields, remember this device, and links to the staff and contractor sign-ins, beside a photo of two workers reviewing a clipboard"
        widths={[800, 1200, 1600, 2200]}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>The member sign-in, with the staff and contractor sign-ins one click away.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">On a phone</p>
    <h2>Built for the phone in a vest</h2>
    <p>
      Every page collapses to a single column below desktop width. The menu folds into a full-width
      panel with large tap targets, and the calendar keeps a full month readable on a phone, with a
      dot on each day that has an event.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <div class="cs-phones">
        {phoneScreens.map((s) => <Screenshot src={s.src} alt={s.alt} widths={[300, 500, 700]} sizes={phoneSizes} class="cs-screen cs-phone" />)}
      </div>
      <figcaption>The home page, the mobile menu, and the calendar on a phone.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Engineering highlights</p>
    <h2>Small rules that keep the site honest</h2>
    <div class="cs-features">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="layers" /></span>
        <h3>Its own design system</h3>
        <p>
          The website has its own visual system, scoped in the same stylesheet as the portals’ so the
          two can’t bleed into each other. Fonts are self-hosted rather than loaded from Google, and
          the pages are prerendered at build time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="compass" /></span>
        <h3>Navigation from one table</h3>
        <p>
          The header and footer read the same site map, so a page can’t be called one thing in the
          menu and another in the footer. A page that hasn’t shipped yet shows as a disabled “not
          published yet” entry instead of a link to a 404.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="file-text" /></span>
        <h3>Content as data</h3>
        <p>
          Locals, events, services, and resources are typed data read through the same data-access
          layer as the portals, so moving any of them behind the API changes one function and no
          page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="check-circle" /></span>
        <h3>Tested like the portals</h3>
        <p>
          The same Playwright suite opens every page and fails on any console error, then signs in
          as each account type and resets a password end to end. CI runs it on every push.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="users" /></span>
        <h3>Accessible by default</h3>
        <p>
          A skip link past the navigation, visible focus rings, labeled fields with the right
          autocomplete hints, carousels and a calendar grid that announce themselves to screen
          readers, and reduced-motion support.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="target" /></span>
        <h3>Honest about what’s missing</h3>
        <p>
          The app store buttons and legal pages exist but are marked “not published yet” until the
          listings and counsel’s text do, rather than pointing at pages that aren’t there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">What’s next</p>
    <h2>What launch still needs</h2>
    <p>
      The site launches on AWS Amplify with the rest of the platform. Before then, the legal pages
      (privacy, HIPAA, terms, nondiscrimination, and accessibility) go live once counsel supplies the
      text, the app store buttons point at real listings, and the calendar and locals move from
      typed data to the API.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
